 "endCard": 65.9667,
 "end": {
  "title": "Next drop: the hammer and the *feather*",
  "follow": "Follow Rest Frame",
  "foot": "Joule, 1850: 772 foot-pounds warm a pound of water 1 °F. Today's value is 778."
 },
 "voice": [
  {
   "id": "vo01",
   "t": 0.1,
   "dur": 4.021,
   "file": "vo/01.wav",
   "text": "Why can't it reach your hand? Somewhere, nearly half its bounce disappeared."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo02",
   "t": 4.666666666666667,
   "dur": 7.95,
   "file": "vo/02.wav",
   "text": "One meter down. Fifty-six centimeters back up. Then thirty-two. Then eighteen. Each bounce keeps just over half its height."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo03",
   "t": 13.166666666666666,
   "dur": 5.804,
   "file": "vo/03.wav",
   "text": "Look closer. The knock: a puff of sound. The ball squashes, and doesn't spring back as hard."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo04",
   "t": 19.733333333333334,
   "dur": 14.252,
   "file": "vo/04.wav",
   "text": "We call that energy lost. James Joule wouldn't. In the eighteen-forties, he let falling weights turn a paddle in water, and the water warmed. Energy doesn't vanish. It becomes heat, shaking and sound, spread too thin to see."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo05",
   "t": 36.3,
   "dur": 8.606,
   "file": "vo/05.wav",
   "text": "Balance it on a basketball. Drop both together. The basketball hands over most of its bounce. The tennis ball rockets past your hand."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo06",
   "t": 48.2,
   "dur": 8.73,
   "file": "vo/06.wav",
   "text": "Measure it: those missing forty-four centimeters? A quarter of a joule. Even if it all stays in the ball: about three thousandths of a degree."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo07",
   "t": 57.63333333333333,
   "dur": 4.189,
   "file": "vo/07.wav",
   "text": "A bounce never destroys energy. It gives it away, knock by knock."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo08",
   "t": 62.7,
   "dur": 3.082,
   "file": "vo/08.wav",
   "text": "Next: a hammer, a feather, the Moon. Nobody wins."
  }
 ]
}